    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d503">

                <pb facs="1330" n="1314" sameAs="frus:frus1951v01/pg1314"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">394.31/4–251: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Acting Chairman of the United States Delegation to
                        the Torquay Conference</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Corse</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">secret</hi>
                    </seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Torquay</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1951-04-02T14:00:00+00:00">April 2, 1951—2 p.
                            m</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>647. After considering latest Australian offers TAC voted terminate negots with
                    Australia on grounds no basis for agrmt exists. Only new item of substantial
                    value was tin plate on which offer was to place present rates in GATT unbound.
                    Del considers tin plate “padding” by nature of product. New offers covered 34
                    tariff subclasses. Of these, 13 were elim primage<anchor n="1" xml:id="d503-f1"
                    /> only; 11 were placing present rates in GATT but unbound; 3 were bindings
                    present rates; 1 was elim primage bind present rate; 6 reductions, 2 of 12½
                    points, 4 of 5 points. Altho offers now cover about 114 tariff subclasses
                    (request list covered 164) shallow cuts in both rates and preferences make
                    offers of little benefit US trade. In addition, Australia adamant refusal
                    relinquish certain agric preferences Canada and UK of importance US negots those
                    countries.</p>
                <p>Informal feelers had indicated previously Australia not interested limited agrmt
                    without wool. Under circumstances, unanimously agreed no possibility
                    satisfactory agrmt.</p>
                <p>Sent Dept 647; repeated info Canberra unnumbered.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Corse</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d503-f1" n="1" xml:id="d503-f1-content">
                <p>In Australia primage was a primary ad valorem revenue duty laid by the
                    Commonwealth Government upon imports.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
